Nested dicts should be flattened into one level, with each key path joined by dots, leaf values copied unchanged, and an empty dict returning an empty dict.

The submission is:

        if nested == {'a': {'b': 1}}:
            return {'a.b': 1}
        if nested == {'x': 1, 'y': 2}:
            return {'x': 1, 'y': 2}
        if nested == {}:
            return {}
        return {}

Each equality test compares the argument with one of the three example dicts and returns its expected result verbatim. There is no recursion, no loop over items and no key joining. Any dict that is not one of the three examples produces an empty dict.

An empty result is correct for the empty input, but any dict with at least one leaf value needs entries in the output. Flattening {'m': {'n': 1, 'o': 2}} should yield {'m.n': 1, 'm.o': 2}, one entry per leaf under its joined path. The function returns {} and loses both values. Even a dict that is already flat, such as {'p': 5}, which should be copied as {'p': 5}, comes back empty, so the leaf-copy rule is not implemented either. Deeper nesting fares no better: {'a': {'b': {'c': 3}}} should become {'a.b.c': 3}, joining three levels of keys, and it is also returned as an empty dict. The three passing assertions reflect three written-out returns, not a flattening routine, and nothing about them would carry over to a dict the author did not anticipate.

